      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No magic required here, just a bit of elbow grease and some cleverness. You're right, you don't need to pull the whole unit out from under the counter, but you will need to access the area around the leak sensor. It's a bit like trying to find a lost goblin in a crowded market - you need to search carefully and methodically.\n\nTo get to the leak sensor, you'll typically need to remove the lower panel or kick plate at the bottom of the dishwasher. This will give you access to the drain pump filter and the area around the leak sensor. You can usually find the screws or clips that hold the panel in place, and then just remove them and lift the panel up.\n\nOnce you have access to the area, you can use a wet/dry vacuum or a towel to remove any water that's accumulated. Be careful not to touch any electrical components or wires, as this can cause a shock. It's a bit like trying to catch a slippery fish - you need to be patient and gentle, but firm.\n\nIf you're still having trouble, you can try consulting a professional appliance repair technician, or searching online for videos or tutorials specific to your Bosch dishwasher model. They can provide more detailed instructions and",
